            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0102" n="92" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg92"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European Advisory Commission/10–344:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-03T18:00:00+01:00">October 3, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 3—4:44 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8273. Department’s 8037, October 2, 7 p.m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d62-f1"/> With
                    reference to the request of the French Committee that its representative be
                    admitted to sit on the European Advisory Commission I am glad to have your
                    comment. Although this matter has never been officially before the EAC and might
                    be favored by the British, I am certain that the Russians would be violently
                    against it and I believe it would be a mistake to introduce any other national
                    element into the Commission until the military phase is established, following
                    the surrender of Germany. The essential task in the EAC at the present time is
                    to reach agreed conclusion on a tripartite basis.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
